OpenAI is changing how paid ChatGPT plans are metered. Its Help Center says that starting in December, Plus, Pro and Business will draw on one shared usage allowance for ChatGPT and Codex, the company's coding agent. If you use chat for emails and Codex or ChatGPT Work for longer jobs, one allowance will matter for both. Here is what OpenAI's pages and one news report say as of 26 November 2026, and what they leave open.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which competes with OpenAI, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test the product and has no affiliate relationship with OpenAI at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What changes in December

OpenAI ties the change to a product move: the agentic features of Work, meaning the parts that carry out multi-step tasks, are moving into the main ChatGPT experience. Alongside that, the allowance on three plan families becomes shared between ChatGPT and Codex.

The main points from the help article are these:

- **Plans named:** Plus, Pro and Business. The article does not mention Free, Go, Enterprise or Edu.
- **Unit:** tokens, the units of information a model processes and generates. The more advanced the model and the harder the task, the more of the allowance it generally uses.
- **Instant chat:** OpenAI states that "Instant chat will not draw from your usage."
- **Tracking:** combined remaining usage and reset times are to be shown in Settings, then Usage.
- **At the limit:** the choices are waiting for the reset, moving to a higher plan or paying for credits that cover eligible features.
- **Timing:** a small group may see the change before December, so the company can learn from feedback before a wider rollout.

The article gives no exact December date, no new allowance figures and no credit prices. OpenAI says it expects most users will not need to wait for resets more often than they do now. That is a forecast about most people, not a promise to every account.

TechCrunch reported on 25 November that the combined experience had begun reaching a small group of Pro subscribers, according to a post by an OpenAI product lead. It also reported a company spokesperson saying some of the very heaviest users could run out earlier. We did not see that statement on an OpenAI page, so treat it as reported.

## Instant is the exception, with a catch

For light users the Instant line is the important one. The help page for Business plans describes Instant chat access as "virtually unlimited", subject to safeguards against misuse, and says this does not make reasoning models, Pro models, Work, Codex or other tools unlimited. It also warns that automatic reasoning may use your reasoning allowance even when Instant is selected. To manage that, the Business page points to Settings, then General, and the Higher intelligence option.

## How today's Work and Codex limits behave

OpenAI has not published December numbers, but its current pages show how the Work and Codex allowance already works for Plus, Pro and Business. Expect details to change.

- Work and Codex already share the plan's included allowance.
- Limits may include a five-hour window and a weekly window, and you need room in both. A new five-hour window starts with your first Work or Codex message after the last one ends.
- The Pro $100, $200 and $500 tiers currently have no five-hour limit in Work and Codex, though a weekly allowance still applies.
- The allowance is not a fixed message count. Model, task size and reasoning effort all affect it.

For Plus and Standard Business seats, OpenAI's pricing page estimates these local messages per five-hour window: GPT-6.2 Astra 6 to 50, GPT-6.2 Sol 17 to 180, GPT-6 Astra 5 to 45, GPT-6.1 Sol 15 to 160, GPT-6 Sol 15 to 150, and GPT-6 Luna 350 to 3,000. It also lists GPT-6.1 Terra at 35 to 285. These are estimates, cloud tasks may use more, and weekly limits may also apply. The Business and Astra help pages we cite do not show 6.2 estimates in their tables. The Business page also lists 15 to 150 for GPT-6.1 Sol, so the pages differ slightly on that figure.

![Range chart on a logarithmic scale of OpenAI's estimated local messages per five-hour window for six models on Plus and Standard Business](/images/site1/chatgpt-codex-shared-usage-limit-december-explained-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

The spread is the lesson. On these estimates, GPT-6 Luna fits far more messages into a window than either Astra model. These are current Work and Codex figures, not a preview of the shared allowance.

## Notes for Business buyers

Business has two seat types, Standard and Premium. Premium includes more Work and Codex usage and has no five-hour limit. Once included usage is gone, paid workspace credits can cover eligible use, subject to spending controls. Without that, you wait for the reset or pick another option.

Chat's GPT-6 Pro model has its own allowance today. The Business page gives Standard seats 15 Pro messages a month and Premium seats 50 a week, shared with GPT-5.6 Sol Pro. The December article does not say how these separate Pro limits fit the shared allowance.

There is also a short offer on that page. Standard and Premium seats in Business workspaces **created from 23 to 30 November 2026** get double weekly included Work and Codex usage through 31 December 2026. The five-hour limit on Standard seats still applies, and existing or reactivated workspaces do not qualify when they add seats or renew. The bonus ends on 31 December, so weigh the usual allowance too.

## What to do before December

1. Open Settings, then Usage, and note how much of your allowance a normal week takes.
2. List which jobs really need the strongest model. OpenAI's guidance suggests lower reasoning effort as a starting point if you want faster replies or to stretch your allowance, since higher effort can use more.
3. Watch Fast mode and very large inputs, which OpenAI says use more allowance.
4. If your team depends on Codex, check how much chat work each Codex user does, since both are due to draw on one allowance. The December article does not describe any pooling between users.
5. Review workspace spending controls, which affect whether credits can be used after included limits.
6. Re-read the help article in early December for dates and numbers.

This is general information, not financial or legal advice. Prices, plan names and limits change often, so confirm them on OpenAI's pages before you pay.

## What is still unknown

We could not find the exact switch date, the size of the new shared allowance, how it compares with today's separate limits, or what happens to Free and Go accounts. Whether you will hit limits sooner depends on how you use the product. The Usage screen is the practical place to check once the change reaches you.
